# Session startup

Run this at the start of every work session. It ensures the environment is current, you've seen any rule updates, and you have a clear plan rooted in the task tracker.

## Steps

### 1. Sync branch with `{{MAIN_BRANCH}}`

Pull first — before anything else. This ensures the rest of startup operates on fresh code.

```
git fetch origin {{MAIN_BRANCH}}
git merge origin/{{MAIN_BRANCH}} --no-edit
```

If conflicts surface, stop and report them. Do not auto-resolve. The user decides.

If new commits arrived, briefly note what changed:

```
git log --oneline ORIG_HEAD..HEAD
```

### 2. Check for updated instructions

If `{{INSTRUCTION_FILE}}` (or any other top-level instruction file) changed since the last session, read the diff and summarize what's new:

```
git diff ORIG_HEAD..HEAD -- {{INSTRUCTION_FILE}}
```

Pay attention to:
- New rules
- Changed commands
- New gotchas or anti-patterns

If nothing changed, note "No instruction updates."

{{IF ARCH_DOC}}
### 3. Refresh architecture context

Read `{{ARCH_DOC}}` at the repo root. It captures the project's current-state architecture — load-bearing constraints, key abstractions, what depends on what.

```
[ -f {{ARCH_DOC}} ] && cat {{ARCH_DOC}}
```

Folder-level `{{ARCH_DOC}}` files (in subdirectories) are loaded on-demand as you work in those folders. If your instinct conflicts with the architecture, follow the doc or raise the conflict with the user before diverging.
{{ENDIF}}

### 3b. Load the findings index

**Read `{{FINDINGS_FILE}}` in full. Every session, without exception.**

```
[ -f {{FINDINGS_FILE}} ] && cat {{FINDINGS_FILE}}
```

`{{ARCH_DOC}}` says how the project is built. This says **what it has learned** — which assumptions were tested, which approaches were ruled out and why, and which previously-stated claims have since been withdrawn. It is capped at {{FINDINGS_CAP}} lines precisely so it can be loaded every time.

Pay particular attention to `✗` entries. Those are claims this project once believed and has since disproved; re-deriving one costs a session. If a finding's `→` reference looks relevant to today's task, open that doc before starting rather than after.

If a finding contradicts what you are about to do, raise it with the user before proceeding — the same rule as the architecture doc.

Do **not** read `{{SESSIONS_FILE}}` at startup. It is an unbounded ledger; opening it defeats the point of the split. Consult it only when you need the history of a specific change.

{{IF PRIOR_WORK_FILE}}
**Then read `{{PRIOR_WORK_FILE}}`, also in full.**

```
[ -f {{PRIOR_WORK_FILE}} ] && cat {{PRIOR_WORK_FILE}}
```

`{{FINDINGS_FILE}}` is what *this project* learned. This is what was already known before it started — one line per source, capped at {{PRIOR_WORK_CAP}}, pointing into `{{PRIOR_WORK_DIR}}/` where the reading lives.

It guards a specific and expensive mistake: **writing a claim from recollection when a source exists.** A plan that asserts what some technique achieves, sourced from nothing but the model's memory of the literature, reads exactly like one grounded in papers actually read — and the difference only surfaces when the number turns out wrong.

Note the provenance markers. `~` means abstract-only, so do not quote a number off that line. `✗` means the literature was searched and does not cover this, or covers it and refutes it — treat a `✗` as a real result, not a gap to fill by guessing.
{{ENDIF}}

{{IF CLAIMS_FILE}}
**Then read `{{CLAIMS_FILE}}`.**

```
[ -f {{CLAIMS_FILE}} ] && cat {{CLAIMS_FILE}}
```

The third of the trio: findings is what the project **learned**, prior work is what was **already known**, this is what the two together let it **assert**. Capped at {{CLAIMS_CAP}}, pointing into `{{CLAIMS_DIR}}/`.

Read the two columns separately, because they answer different questions. **Novelty** is whether a reviewer would call it new; **evidence** is whether we can show it. A row that is `✗ absent` on novelty and `○ asserted` on evidence is wide open *and* unearned — the most tempting square on the board and the weakest.

Two markers to act on. **`?` unsearched is a liability**, not a blank claim of novelty: it means nobody has checked, and searching can only lower it — so a `?` on a row today's work touches is a cheap thing to fix. And **if today's work would move a row, it moves at wrap-up, with the commit in the cell** — never on the strength of the session's own enthusiasm.
{{ENDIF}}

{{IF INFRA_LAUNCH}}
### 4. Launch project infrastructure

Run any project-specific bootstrap:

```
{{INFRA_LAUNCH}}
```

This should be idempotent — safe to call when already started. If it isn't, fix the script before continuing.
{{ENDIF}}

### 5. Resume or claim the task

**First, check for an already-in-progress task** (the prior session's `wrap-up` may have reserved one for resumption):

{{IF TASK_SOURCE_GITHUB}}
```
gh issue list {{IF AGENT_LABEL}}--label {{AGENT_LABEL}} {{ENDIF}}--label {{LABEL_IN_PROGRESS}} --state open
```
{{ENDIF}}{{IF TASK_SOURCE_TASKS}}
```
{{TASKS_HELPER}} list --status {{STATUS_IN_PROGRESS}} --owner @me
```
{{ENDIF}}

If multiple in-progress tasks exist, ask the user which to resume (use `AskUserQuestion`).

**If exactly one in-progress task is found, run the project's `claim` skill on it** — `/claim <id>`. The claim flow is idempotent: the status flip step is a no-op when the task is already in-progress (a `wrap-up`-reserved task), but {{IF ARCH_DOC}}the architecture refresh, {{ENDIF}}clarifying questions{{IF TASK_SOURCE_GITHUB}}, and plan comment{{ENDIF}} still run with fresh attention. This is the recommended path: `wrap-up` reserves; `startup` completes the claim.

**If no in-progress task exists**, pick from cold via the project's `claim` skill:

{{IF TASK_SOURCE_GITHUB}}
- Available to pick up: `gh issue list --label {{LABEL_READY}} --state open`
- Then `/claim <id>` to run the full claim flow
{{ENDIF}}{{IF TASK_SOURCE_TASKS}}
- Available to pick up: `{{TASKS_HELPER}} list --status {{STATUS_READY}}`
- Or auto-pick the highest-priority ready task: `{{TASKS_HELPER}} next`
- Then `/claim <id>` to run the full claim flow
{{ENDIF}}

### 6. Report ready and begin

Summarize the session state for the user:

- Branch name and sync status
- Any instruction changes they should know about
- The task you're working on (id + title)

Then continue with the project's standard workstream — typically Phase 2 (clarify-and-challenge) or Phase 4 (design — `wovenflow:designflow`).

## Anti-patterns

- **Skipping the sync** — operating on stale code wastes the entire session when a merge surfaces a fundamental change
- **Auto-resolving merge conflicts** — they signal divergent assumptions; the user decides
- **Skipping the instruction diff** — silently violating new rules is worse than asking
{{IF ARCH_DOC}}- **Skipping the architecture refresh** — orientation drift; suggestions will increasingly conflict with the project's actual shape
{{ENDIF}}- **Treating a reserved task as fully claimed** — when wrap-up reserved a task, run `/claim <id>` at startup so {{IF ARCH_DOC}}the architecture refresh, {{ENDIF}}clarifying questions{{IF TASK_SOURCE_GITHUB}}, and plan comment{{ENDIF}} happen with fresh attention. The claim skill is idempotent on the status flip.
